WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.775
Source ingestion is the first architectural control point in private RAG.

2
00:00:04.775 --> 00:00:16.449
The system may ingest files, wikis, knowledge bases, tickets, source repositories, policy libraries, transcripts, shared drives, structured records, or other internal sources.

3
00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:24.939
Each source arrives with its own owner, permission model, quality level, update pattern, retention expectation, and sensitivity.

4
00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:31.079
A private RAG pipeline should not flatten those differences into one uncontrolled pile of text.

5
00:00:31.079 --> 00:00:44.571
During ingestion, the system should preserve the source system, document owner, classification, access rules, ingestion date, modified date, retention status, lifecycle state, and deletion expectations.

6
00:00:44.571 --> 00:00:46.694
Those details are not paperwork.

7
00:00:46.694 --> 00:00:50.484
They are the data controls that later retrieval depends on.

8
00:00:50.484 --> 00:00:52.985
Boundaries also exist inside content.

9
00:00:52.985 --> 00:01:02.081
A document may contain sections, tables, appendices, exceptions, regional instructions, tenant-specific clauses, or archived material.

10
00:01:02.081 --> 00:01:06.402
A shared repository may mix public guidance with restricted notes.

11
00:01:06.402 --> 00:01:10.192
A policy library may contain current and retired versions.

12
00:01:10.192 --> 00:01:16.332
Ingestion should keep those distinctions visible enough for chunking, filtering, and review.

13
00:01:16.332 --> 00:01:21.411
Data normalization is useful when it makes content searchable and consistent.

14
00:01:21.411 --> 00:01:28.915
Boundary loss is dangerous when it removes ownership, classification, or the meaning of where a statement came from.

15
00:01:28.915 --> 00:01:36.571
The goal is to prepare content for retrieval without stripping away the rules that made the original source governable.

16
00:01:36.571 --> 00:01:39.754
Private RAG starts by respecting source systems.

17
00:01:39.754 --> 00:01:49.912
If a document is expired, confidential, tenant-specific, archived, or restricted before ingestion, it should remain recognizable as such after ingestion.

18
00:01:49.912 --> 00:01:56.886
Carrying that meaning forward makes retrieval safer, review easier, and deletion workflows more reliable.

19
00:01:56.886 --> 00:01:59.387
Treat ingestion as a governed handoff.

20
00:01:59.387 --> 00:02:12.197
Before content enters the RAG pipeline, the team should know who approved it, what connector touched it, what transformations occurred, and what evidence would be needed to remove or correct it later.
